course: world
lesson: 92
title: The human life cycle and growing up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every person starts as a single tiny cell that grows inside the mother's womb, a special place in her belly, for about nine months before birth.
explain: After birth a person passes through stages: baby, toddler, child, teenager, adult and old age; at each stage the body and the mind change.
explain: Babies grow faster than at any other time: most babies roughly triple their weight in their first year, and their brains grow very quickly as they learn to move, understand and speak.
explain: Puberty, which usually starts somewhere between about 8 and 14, is when the body changes into an adult body; the bones grow quickly and then stop getting longer by the late teens or early twenties.
explain: Example: you have features from both your parents — perhaps your mother's eyes and your father's hair — because you get instructions called genes from each of them.
fact: A baby grows inside its mother's womb for about nine months before it is born.
fact: The stages of human life are baby, toddler, child, teenager, adult and old age.
fact: Most babies roughly triple their birth weight in their first year.
fact: A newborn baby has more bones than an adult, because some bones join together as we grow.
fact: Children have 20 baby teeth, and most adults have 32 permanent teeth.
fact: Puberty is the time when a child's body changes into an adult's body.
fact: Puberty starts at different ages for different people, and girls often start earlier than boys.
fact: People stop growing taller when the growth areas at the ends of their long bones close, usually in the late teens or early twenties.
fact: Children get genes from both parents, which is why they look a little like each of them.
fact: As people grow old their skin becomes less stretchy and their bones can become weaker.
fact: Humans need adults to care for them for many years, much longer than most animals.
exercise: How long does a baby usually grow inside its mother before birth? | About nine months | About three months | About two years | About two weeks
exercise: Which stage comes right after childhood? | The teenage years | Old age and retirement | Being a little toddler | Being a newborn baby
exercise: When does a person grow fastest? | As a baby | As an adult | In old age | All stages grow at the same speed
exercise: How many baby teeth does a child have? | 20 | 32 | 10 | 44
exercise: How many permanent teeth do most adults have? | 32 | 20 | 28 at most | 50
exercise: Who has more bones, a newborn baby or an adult? | A newborn baby | An adult | They have exactly the same number | Babies have no real bones
exercise: Why does a baby have more bones than an adult? | Some bones join together as we grow | Adults lose bones in accidents | Bones dissolve in old age | Babies grow extra bones and then drop them
exercise: What is puberty? | The time when a child's body changes into an adult's | The time when a baby learns to walk | The time when old people stop growing | The time when baby teeth grow in
exercise: Why does a teenager stop getting taller? | The growth areas at the ends of the bones close | The person stops eating enough | The bones run out of calcium for ever | The heart stops sending blood to the legs
exercise: Why does a child look a little like both parents? | The child gets genes from each parent | The child copies the parents' faces | The child eats the same food | The parents choose how the child will look
exercise: Which change often happens in old age? | Bones can become weaker | People grow much taller | Baby teeth grow back | The heart stops needing oxygen
exercise: What do babies learn very quickly in their first years? | To move, understand and speak | To read and write very long books | To drive a car and go to work | To cook all of their own meals
exercise: Do all children start puberty at the same age? | No, it starts at different ages for different people | Yes, everyone starts on their 12th birthday | Yes, but only for boys | No, only some people go through it
test: Max is 11 and his friend is the same age but much taller. What does this most likely show? | People grow and change at different paces | Max must be unhealthy and eating badly at home | His friend must really be a lot older than him | Max will never grow any taller than he is now
test: Which is true? | A baby grows faster in its first year than an adult ever does | Adults keep growing taller every year | Newborns have fewer bones than adults | Everyone starts puberty at exactly 13
test: A 25-year-old wants to grow 10 centimetres taller by stretching. Why will it not work? | The growth areas in her bones have closed | She is stretching the wrong muscles | She needs to stretch for much longer each day | Adults grow only when they sleep less
test: A girl has her grandmother's curly hair. How is this possible? | Genes passed down through her parents | She chose curly hair as a baby | Her hair copied her grandmother's | She ate the same food as her grandmother
test: A 7-year-old loses a front tooth. What happens next? | A permanent tooth grows in its place | No tooth will ever grow in that gap again | The same baby tooth grows back again later | A third set of teeth begins to grow in
test: Put the stages in order. | Baby, toddler, child, teenager, adult | Child, baby, toddler, adult, teenager | Toddler, baby, teenager, child, adult | Baby, child, toddler, adult, teenager
test: Why does a human child need care from adults for so long? | Humans take many years to grow and learn | Humans are born able to look after themselves | Children cannot learn anything new | Adults need children to feed them
test: A baby weighed 3 kilograms at birth. About what might it weigh at one year? | About 9 kilograms | About 3 kilograms | About 30 kilograms | About 1 kilogram
test: Grandpa says his bones break more easily than when he was young. Why? | Bones can become weaker in old age | Old bones are made of different stuff | His bones have grown too long | Bones get harder and stronger with age
test: why's the baby got gaps in its mouth, where's the teeth | Babies grow their 20 baby teeth later, over the first years | Babies have all their teeth hidden in their cheeks | Babies only get teeth when they are teenagers | Babies have 32 teeth that are too small to see
test: why do I look a bit like mum and a bit like dad | You get genes from both parents | You get all your genes from your mum | You copy their faces as you grow up | You get genes only from your grandparents
test: Leo says everyone starts puberty at exactly the same age. true? | No, it starts at different ages, often earlier in girls | Yes, everyone starts on their 13th birthday, no matter what | Yes, but boys always start first | No, some people never go through it
test: my sister reckons a baby has fewer bones than an adult. right? | No, a newborn has more bones, and some join together as we grow | Yes, babies grow new bones every year | Yes, adults have twice as many bones | No, babies and adults have exactly the same
exam: Ascertain the approximate period for which a baby grows inside its mother's womb. | About nine months | About three months | About eighteen months | About six weeks
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the number of teeth of children and adults? | Children have 20 baby teeth and most adults 32 permanent teeth | Children have 32 baby teeth and most adults 20 permanent teeth | Children and adults alike have 28 teeth | Children have 10 baby teeth and adults 20 teeth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a newborn baby has more bones than an adult. | Certain bones join together in the course of growth | Certain bones are lost entirely in childhood | Adults grow no new bones after birth | Babies possess a second skeleton
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the commencement of puberty. | It starts at different ages, girls often earlier than boys | It starts at exactly the same age in all persons | It starts invariably earlier in boys than in girls | It starts only after the age of twenty
